You know, what you did was right, what she did was wrong. Exactly. You work with couples in your practice. What is your approach? I mean, how do you deal with people who come to you because there has been infidelity in the relationship? So when I work with a couple who has had infidelity in therapy, I use a model that was developed by colleagues of mine, Christy Gordon, Don Bacombe, and Doug Snyder. And there are three stages of that treatment approach. It includes the impact stage, or I kind of think of that as containing the damage and the affair.

29
00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:49.000
Think about who you're going to talk to about the affair, if anyone, right, so that you're not telling your mother and then your mother's telling her friends and this is getting more and more out of control, right? But think about what do you need as a couple to get support that you need, but also be sure that, you know, you're not totally erratic in how you're. you're responding to the news of the affair. The second stage is really understanding why the affair happened. So getting at some of those things you and I have talked about today around motivations, access to another partner, what it meant to the participating partner, what it feels like to the injured partner, the person who was hurt by the affair, of really understanding it.

30
00:23:49.000 --> 00:24:12.000
So that phase can be really difficult for couples to move through because they've got to talk about the affair, the impact it had, what was happening in the relationship that they may need to change if they decide to move forward together that kind of allowed the affair to happen.

